1. THE FORGETTING CURVE: R = e^(-t/S), WITH S = 1 DAY
   after  0 days  recall 100.0%  ########################################
   after  1 days  recall  36.8%  ###############
   after  2 days  recall  13.5%  #####
   after  3 days  recall   5.0%  ##
   after  7 days  recall   0.1%  
   after 30 days  recall   0.0%  
   Half is gone in less than a day. Without a review, a fact met once is
   as good as lost within a week.

2. FOUR REVIEWS EACH, THE SAME EFFORT, DIFFERENT TIMING (toy model)
   plan                                           day  1 day  7 day 30 day 90   stability
   crammed: four times on day 0, an hour apart     56.2%   1.8%   0.0%   0.0%      1.7 days
   spaced: days 0, 1, 3 and 7                      97.2%  82.1%  43.1%   8.0%     35.6 days
   spaced wider: days 0, 2, 7 and 16               98.6%  90.8%  66.2%  29.0%     72.6 days
   (Each test day is counted from the last review, so no plan gets credit for
   having reviewed later.) Cramming feels productive because every re-read
   succeeds, and that is exactly why it teaches the memory little: it was never
   in danger. Spacing lets a fact fade a little before each review.

3. HOW A FLASHCARD PROGRAM SPACES REVIEWS: SM-2 (Wozniak, 1987)
   First gap 1 day, second gap 6 days, then each gap is the last one x 2.5,
   rounded up, for a card you keep answering right.
   review  1   gap     1 days   on day     1  (year  0.0)
   review  2   gap     6 days   on day     7  (year  0.0)
   review  3   gap    15 days   on day    22  (year  0.1)
   review  4   gap    38 days   on day    60  (year  0.2)
   review  5   gap    95 days   on day   155  (year  0.4)
   review  6   gap   238 days   on day   393  (year  1.1)
   review  7   gap   595 days   on day   988  (year  2.7)
   review  8   gap  1488 days   on day  2476  (year  6.8)
   review  9   gap  3720 days   on day  6196  (year 17.0)

4. THE COST OF REMEMBERING GROWS LIKE A LOGARITHM
   After the first two reviews the gaps grow by a factor 2.5, so the days
   covered form a geometric series: 7 + 15 + 37.5 + ... ~ 7 + 15(2.5^k - 1)/1.5.
   Keeping a card for T days therefore takes about log(T) / log(2.5) reviews:
   keep it for | SM-2 reviews | one review a week
        1 year |            6 |                53
       2 years |            7 |               105
       5 years |            8 |               261
      10 years |            9 |               522
   Keeping a fact 2.5 times as long costs one more review, not 2.5 times as many.
   For a deck of 1000 cards kept a year: 16 reviews a day on SM-2,
   145 a day on a weekly round. That is the arithmetic behind the Anki
   decks in this library.
